<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yeavering</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ad Gefrin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yeavering, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, is the site of Ad Gefrin, a royal residence of the 7th-century kings of Bernicia and Northumbria below the hillfort of Yeavering Bell. Excavations from 1953 revealed great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> halls and a wedge-shaped wooden grandstand for assemblies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> records that the missionary Paulinus baptised people in the River Glen there for 36 days around 627.</p></div>
